# Supply-chain hardening (gate-84). THREE settings that only work together;
# any one alone is a configuration that looks like protection and is not:
#
#   1. min-release-age            — the cooldown window itself
#   2. min-release-age-exclude[]  — first-party releases must NOT be delayed
#   3. package.json engines.npm   — npm 10 does NOT implement min-release-age
#
# `min-release-age=0` was the previous value here, under a comment claiming it
# rejected anything published less than 24h ago. Both halves were wrong: the
# unit is DAYS, not hours, and `0` is not a shortened window — it is a cooldown
# of ZERO, fully inert while looking configured.
#
# Without the exclusion the cooldown does not fail loudly — it silently
# resolves BACKWARDS. Measured 2026-08-15: installing @conduction/nextcloud-vue
# on release day under a cooldown with no exclusion picked 2.0.7 instead of
# 2.3.0 and exited 0. A green install of months-old first-party code is worse
# than a red one, because nothing distinguishes it from a correct install.
# Only the named packages are exempt; their own dependencies still follow the
# policy.
#
# npm 11+ ONLY. On npm 10 `min-release-age` does not exist — `npm config get
# min-release-age` answers `undefined` — so this file is read by NOTHING on
# that toolchain. `engines.npm: ^11.0.0` in package.json declares the floor,
# which is why gate-84 checks all three together.
min-release-age=2
min-release-age-exclude[]=@conduction/*
